      simple: 'A comprehensive guide to active and passive voice.',
      blocks: [
        { type: 'text', body: 'Voice refers to the relationship between the subject of the sentence and the action (the verb). In the active voice, the subject performs the action. In the passive voice, the subject receives the action. Active voice is generally preferred in writing because it is more direct, concise, and vigorous.' },
        { type: 'example', body: 'Active: The chef prepared a delicious meal. (The subject "chef" is performing the action "prepared".)' },
        { type: 'example', body: 'Passive: A delicious meal was prepared by the chef. (The subject "meal" is receiving the action "was prepared".)' },
        { type: 'text', body: 'While active voice is often stronger, passive voice is necessary and highly effective in specific situations: when the actor is unknown or irrelevant ("The building was constructed in 1920"), when you want to emphasize the recipient of the action ("The suspect was arrested"), or in scientific writing where the focus is on the process rather than the researcher.' },
        { type: 'text', body: 'To form the passive voice, use a form of the "to be" verb (is, are, am, was, were, has been, have been, had been, will be, will have been, being) followed by a past participle.' },
        { type: 'keyTerms', terms: [{term: 'Active Voice', def: 'Subject performs action'}, {term: 'Passive Voice', def: 'Subject receives action'}] },
        { type: 'summary', points: ['Mastering active and passive voice allows writers to control the focus, tone, and pacing of their prose. Use active voice for clarity and impact; use passive voice deliberately for strategic emphasis.'] }
