      ],
      simple: 'A comprehensive guide to parallel structure.',
      blocks: [
        { type: 'text', body: 'Parallel structure (or parallelism) means using the same pattern of words to show that two or more ideas have the same level of importance. This can happen at the word, phrase, or clause level. Parallelism provides balance, rhythm, and clarity to writing.' },
        { type: 'example', body: 'Not Parallel: Mary likes hiking, swimming, and to ride a bicycle.' },
        { type: 'example', body: 'Parallel: Mary likes hiking, swimming, and riding a bicycle. (All verbs are gerunds: -ing form)' },
        { type: 'text', body: 'Parallelism is required in several specific situations: with elements joined by coordinating conjunctions (and, but, or), with elements in lists or series, and with correlative conjunctions (either/or, neither/nor, not only/but also).' },
        { type: 'example', body: 'Correlative Conjunctions: My dog not only likes to play fetch but also likes to chase cars. (The phrase after "not only" parallels the phrase after "but also".)' },
        { type: 'text', body: 'When comparing items, the items being compared must be parallel. "Swimming in the ocean is much tougher than a pool." is incorrect. It should be "Swimming in the ocean is much tougher than swimming in a pool."' },
        { type: 'keyTerms', terms: [{ term: 'Parallelism', def: 'The repetition of a chosen grammatical form within a sentence.' }, { term: 'Correlative Conjunctions', def: 'Pairs of conjunctions that coordinate two items (e.g. }, { term: both/and, def: either/or).' }] },
        { type: 'summary', points: ['Parallel structure creates smoother, more readable sentences. Always check lists, comparisons, and paired conjunctions to ensure grammatical consistency.'] }
